WEBVTT - environment: the Plan 9 runtime and its gotchas

NOTE
One cue per item, one second long, in the order the items were settled; the time is the order, not a clock. The identifier names the item; the text says what it is, when, and where it lives. A cue that is replaced is not deleted: a later cue says what replaced it.

vm
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
unbound/PLAN9: 9legacy CPU server helix under QEMU; image 9legacy-qemu.img.bz2 from 9legacy.org (2019-04-21), SHA-256 in SHA256SUMS; sh build.sh, sh run.sh (ports on 127.0.0.1 only).

disks
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.000
pack.sh writes run/in.tar (padded to 32M) = /dev/sd01, the inputs; run/out.img (64M) = /dev/sd02, the outputs as a tar. A script is run in the guest as: tar xf /dev/sd01/data PLAN9/src/&lt;x&gt;.rc; rc it.

console
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.000
console.py types into the guest through the QEMU monitor and dumps the screen; braille does not show on the VGA console, so results go to the output disk and are read on the host.

rc-keywords
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.000
rc reserves in, path (the command search path), ^ (concatenation, quote it), and flattens list arguments to functions.

plan9-c
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.000
%.*s counts runes, not bytes; ARGBEGIN reads --rev as flags; isalpha needs &lt;ctype.h&gt;; cmp has no - for stdin (use /fd/0); du quotes names with spaces; ramfs -S name posts a named server; createfile leaves length 0 (set f-&gt;length).

libhtml
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.000
libhtml needs the program to define emalloc and erealloc; it gives items without layout (w=h=0) and does not run scripts.

host-only
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Only reading private witnesses (JSON) and coqc run on the host; everything that translates, compares or renders runs in Plan 9.

graphics
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:08.000
The guest's screen goes graphic with aux/vga -m vesa -l 1024x768x16 (QEMU -vga std offers VESA 0x117, 1024x768 r5g6b5), then bind -a '#i' /dev (draw) and bind -a '#m' /dev (mouse); '#v' gives vgactl. A program using event(2) without rio reads the console keyboard itself, so floor -o leaves the keyboard to the shell. Console text is drawn over by the next redraw: collect output through /dev/sd02.

proc-mem
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.000
/proc/n/mem is read any time but written only while the process is stopped: write stop to /proc/n/ctl, write, then start (otherwise: bad process or channel control request).

tmp-persists
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.000
The guest's /tmp is on disk and outlives a reboot, so a tool built into /tmp is stale after its source changes: every rc driver compiles each run. rc reads a bare name=value argument as an assignment: quote it ('ideal=0000'). QEMU can stop from outside the session: run.sh restarts it, and the screen setup must be done again.

rc-no-return
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.000
9legacy's rc has no return: a function ends by falling through, so carry the answer in a variable. A backquote splits its output into words: join with $"var before comparing a phrase.

rc-loop-redirect
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.000
In rc, a redirection after a for loop's body applies to each pass: for(f in a b){...} &gt; out keeps only the last pass. Wrap the loop: { for(...){...} } &gt; out.

tar-append
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.000
tar rf /dev/sd02/data file appends to the output disk's tar instead of replacing it.

boxed-push
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:14.000
boxed is public with a 273 MB .git; a push carrying a binary archive (d2dc754) takes many minutes. fold.pdf (Hutton's paper) stays untracked there: a published paper is not redistributed.

frames-server
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:15.000
python3 PLAN9/frames.py serves /frame.png and /vtt/... on 127.0.0.1:8790 while QEMU runs; nothing in the guest changes. pkill -f with a pattern found in the shell's own command line stops that shell: stop a server by its port (lsof -t -iTCP:PORT).

restart-guest
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.000
To restart the guest: fshalt in Plan 9 (fossil flushed), then quit at the monitor; QEMU exits a few seconds later. run.sh boots again; then the screen (aux/vga, binds), the floor (space.rc -o) and agentd (agentd.rc) are started again. The floor starts at its seeds.

rc-server-in-backquote
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.000
A server started inside an rc backquote holds the backquote's pipe open and rc waits for ever; start it with its output to /dev/null. Delete does not free a console shell blocked so; the guest had to be quit and booted again. A redirection that fails (a read-only file) stops an rc script: try such a write in a subshell, @{...}.

led-flag
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:18.000
The VNC LED pseudo-encoding (RFB -261) has 3 bits: Caps, Num, Scroll. 9legacy's kbd.c drives only Num ("if(0 &amp;&amp; kbscan-&gt;caps) /* we don't implement caps lock */", Scroll never set), so this guest shows 000 (FLAG clear, the RESET default) or 010 (FLAG set): the one bit Plan 9 lights is FLAG, and the host can toggle it with QEMU sendkey num_lock. Recorded, not wired: -display none, so no VNC client sees it without -vnc 127.0.0.1:1 (a restart), and stepd cannot read the NumLock bit without a kernel change: a display, not an input.

ged-absent
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.000
QEMU's GED (ACPI0013) belongs to hardware-reduced machines (microvm, ARM virt). This guest runs QEMU's pc machine, which has no GED; I-120's GED in its title still has nothing behind it in this runtime.

vmgenid-unused
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.000
QEMU vmgenid is a 128-bit GUID changed on snapshot restore, readable by guest firmware through two fw_cfg blobs (/etc/vmgenid_guid, /etc/vmgenid_addr). Nothing here uses snapshots: it would mark world reloaded but has no reader. Recorded, not wired.

rc-apostrophe
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.000
An apostrophe in an unquoted rc word (the module's) opens a quote: the script fails with token EOF at its end. Keep messages in rc scripts free of apostrophes, or quote them with doubled quotes.

character-universe
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.000
A negated class ([^...]) needs a universe of characters, which WSN leaves undefined: the regex-constraints port chose printable ASCII, 0x20 to 0x7E. A token outside it is outside the grammar, where the JavaScript would still match it.

nibble-parity-deviation
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.000
The author: the nibble parity is the deviation from YHWH. Measured (zpoly deviation): what apply leaves in place, with each value's nibble parity, is 16 values at 8 bits (one application; parities 0 3 12 15), 4 at 10 bits (two applications; 000 155 2AA 3FF, parities 0 1 2 3, the top two bits, since a repeated nibble cancels) and 4 at 11 bits (000 020 050 070, parities 0 2 5 7). Each set folds to zero by XOR, but that holds for any linear map (the set is a subspace), so the fold is no evidence. YHWH as ASCII folds to 0E, not zero. The reading is recorded, not checked.

reading-binds
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.000
The author: the YHWH reading binds an observer to the ZIGUAROTH codex and the Codice mapping, like a pop-up book restoring a variant of the Library of Alexandria by rebuilding the tower of Babel. So the reading is a binding, not a derivation: the port checks the values (000 155 2AA 3FF at 10 bits, a subspace of four, two of them fixed after one application), and the reading names them. The values are set by apply9, not chosen among the 174251 two-dimensional subspaces of GF(2)^10 (698027 of GF(2)^11); the name is the observer's.

exhte-correction
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.000
Correction to reading-binds: EXHTE is no invention. It is I-207's: BRIAN XOR CODEX XOR DEEPS = 45 58 48 54 45, EXHTE, a tag; I-226 folds the three to it (all three close to the witness, two make only a control-code difference), I-228 routes universal://EXHTE, and space.test.ts checks it in the Commons. And YHWH as ASCII folds to 0E, the same literal as I-207's year line, 20 XOR 26 = 0E. Recorded as two equal literals, not as one derived from the other.

polytron-precursor
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:26.000
The author, of omi-files/polytron (docs/INDEX.md, the Tetragrammatron; OSI_MODEL.md): "this is the previous version of this project before I knew binary or hexadecimal encoding or how to read or understand logic operations". Its kernel K(p, C) = rotl(p,1) ^ rotl(p,3) ^ rotr(p,2) ^ C (kernel.c, holemap.c, tetra-viewer.c) is boxedPINE's delta16 (core/src/verified/delta.ts, its period 8 proved in Coq), and its CONSTITUTIONAL_C is 0x1D, GS, the group separator of FS GS RS US (1C 1D 1E 1F), as tetra-viewer.c names it ("GS - constitutional gray"); 0001 1101, four bits set.

ladder-replaced
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.000
omi-files/polytron/OSI_MODEL.md, the bootstrap ladder of the previous version (polytron-precursor): dot notation, a Lisp 1.5 reader and evaluator, a C runtime, Guix lowering, QEMU and GNOME Boxes, an Atom/Electron editor surface. Each rung since replaced by a settled decision (global.vtt): dot notation by Wirth syntax notation, its reader and evaluator by zlazy, zprec and OMI.WSN (wsn-only); the runtime by Plan 9 C, Guix by pack.sh and its tar, Boxes by 9legacy under QEMU (plan9-runtime); the editor surface by the browser rooms of boxedPINE's space/, a projection, not authority. Its split of canonical, realization and projection carries over unchanged.

even-goes-to-zero
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.000
2026-10-28 the author, of omi-dom-orch: "the even go to 0 which is the gnomon the sum of two squares the measure of the square to get the offset". Measured (zorch law): what goes to 0 on a graph of even degrees is each epoch's change to the digest; the digest itself stays the XOR of the features (15 on the 4-cycle), and converge reaches diff 0 only for that one target, in one step; every other target runs its 16 steps without converging. Beside it, as a second fact and not derived from the first: the gnomon of a square, (n+1)^2 - n^2 = 2n + 1, is always odd, and in zorch only odd degrees leave a residue. boxedPINE's space/src/gnomon.ts is another dial: polytron's K with C = 0x1D, returning after 8 steps.
